  "worked_solution": "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0.3)$ | $0.3$ | $(0.3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈-0.3.$ So ${f}^{″}(-0.3)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(-0.3)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈-0.3,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14202834/assets/b1b048d1b6c430f023c0419d7c8442740b76b5d7e646f32fc870be9225649b70.png)",
